From 2e4e15a6a55cad83651081117655158d06883995 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <tor@1aeo.com>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 03:24:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Send each socket's name to the queue only once

Since the port to Python 3 (4d678bc), _Torsocket.__init__() replaces
PySocks' SOCKS5 negotiation function with ourneg(), which sends the
socket's name to the queue before it calls the original function, so
that the event handler can attach the new stream to the probe's
circuit.  But _proxy_negotiators is a class attribute of
socks.socksocket, which all sockets share.  Every new socket thus wraps
the ourneg() of the socket created before it, and the n-th socket of a
probe process sends its name n times.

The Attacher pairs each message with Tor's STREAM NEW event for the
same source port.  For the second connection of a probe, the second
message usually finds the entry the first one left, and exitmap tries
to attach stream "None", which fails with 'Unknown stream "None"'.  The
stream which Tor reports afterwards is never attached, and the
connection fails with "0x06: TTL expired" once Tor's SocksTimeout of
two minutes has passed, unless the module gives up earlier.  The
fourth, sixth, etc. connection can fail in the same way, depending on
when Tor's event arrives.

This affects every module that opens more than one connection in a
probe, e.g., to fetch several targets over one exit relay as in #46, or
to follow a redirect.  It is what the log in !5 shows once that module
fetched several targets per exit relay: "Attempting to attach stream
None to circuit 4."  rtt, which opens ten connections per destination,
only records its first connection per exit relay.  The DNS modules are
not affected, as resolve() sends the socket's name itself.

Give every socket its own copy of the dict, so that it wraps PySocks'
function and sends its name once.

Relates to #46.
---
 src/torsocks.py       |  3 +++
 test/test_torsocks.py | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 19 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/torsocks.py b/src/torsocks.py
index fa229de..653cf6a 100644
--- a/src/torsocks.py
+++ b/src/torsocks.py
@@ -80,6 +80,9 @@ def send_queue(sock_name):
 class _Torsocket(socks.socksocket):
     def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):
         super(_Torsocket, self).__init__(*args, **kwargs)
+        # PySocks shares this dict among all sockets.  Use a copy, or every
+        # new socket would wrap the ourneg() of the previous one.
+        self._proxy_negotiators = dict(self._proxy_negotiators)
         orig_neg = self._proxy_negotiators[2]  # This is the original function
 
         def ourneg(*args, **kwargs):
diff --git a/test/test_torsocks.py b/test/test_torsocks.py
index b905fcb..c2e475f 100644
--- a/test/test_torsocks.py
+++ b/test/test_torsocks.py
@@ -24,6 +24,7 @@ import socket
 import sys
 
 import pytest
+import socks
 
 sys.path.insert(0, 'src/')
 import torsocks
@@ -43,6 +44,21 @@ def test__torsocket():
         _ = t.resolve("localhost")
 
 
+def test__torsocket_send_queue(monkeypatch):
+    # Every socket must send its name to the queue once, no matter how many
+    # sockets were created before.
+    sent = []
+    monkeypatch.setattr(torsocks, "send_queue", sent.append)
+    # Leave out the SOCKS handshake, there is no Tor to talk to.
+    monkeypatch.setitem(socks.socksocket._proxy_negotiators, socks.SOCKS5,
+                        lambda *args: None)
+    for _ in range(3):
+        with torsocks._Torsocket() as t:
+            # PySocks' connect() calls this once it is connected to the proxy.
+            t._proxy_negotiators[socks.SOCKS5](t, "192.0.2.1", 80)
+    assert len(sent) == 3
+
+
 def test_torsocket():
     ts = torsocks.torsocket()
     assert isinstance(ts, torsocks._Torsocket)
